        <h1 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-foreground mb-8 leading-tight">What is GEO (Generative Engine Optimisation) and Why It Matters for Australian Businesses</h1>
        <div class="prose prose-invert prose-lg max-w-none font-sans text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
          
          <p>If you've been following digital marketing in 2025 and 2026, you've probably encountered the acronyms SEO, AEO, and GEO used interchangeably — and often incorrectly. This article explains what Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO) actually is, how it differs from traditional SEO and Answer Engine Optimisation (AEO), and what Australian businesses should be doing about it.</p>
          <h2>The Three Layers of Search Optimisation</h2>
          <p><strong>SEO (Search Engine Optimisation)</strong> is the practice of making your website rank higher in traditional search engine results pages (SERPs) — the blue links on Google. It involves technical optimisation, content quality, and backlink acquisition. SEO has been the dominant digital marketing discipline for 25 years.</p>
          <p><strong>AEO (Answer Engine Optimisation)</strong> is the practice of structuring your content so that it gets cited in direct answers — the featured snippets, knowledge panels, and "People Also Ask" boxes that appear above the traditional blue links. AEO became important as Google shifted toward providing direct answers rather than just links.</p>
          <p><strong>GEO (Generative Engine Optimisation)</strong> is the newest layer. It's the practice of structuring your content so that it gets cited by AI-powered answer engines — ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews, Microsoft Copilot, and similar tools. When someone asks ChatGPT "what's the best predictive AI company in Australia?" or asks Perplexity "how much does AI automation cost in Sydney?", GEO is what determines whether your business appears in the answer.</p>
          <h2>Why GEO Is Different from SEO and AEO</h2>
          <p>Traditional SEO optimises for ranking algorithms that evaluate pages based on keywords, backlinks, and technical signals. AEO optimises for structured data and featured snippet eligibility. GEO optimises for something different: the likelihood that an AI model will cite your content as a credible, authoritative source when generating an answer.</p>
          <p>AI models don't rank pages — they synthesise information from multiple sources and generate responses. To appear in those responses, your content needs to be: factually accurate and specific (AI models penalise vague or unsubstantiated claims), structured in a way that's easy to extract and cite (Q&A format, clear headings, specific data points), and present on domains that AI crawlers have indexed and trust.</p>
          <h2>What Australian Businesses Should Do</h2>
          <p>The businesses that will win in AI search over the next three years are the ones that build a library of high-quality, specific, factual content that answers the questions their target customers are asking AI engines. This is exactly what PresciaIQ's <a href="/answers" class="text-primary hover:underline">AI Answers hub</a> is designed to do — 159 structured Q&A pages targeting the specific questions Australian business owners ask about predictive AI, AEO, and AI automation.</p>
          <p>If you want to build a GEO strategy for your business, <a href="/start" class="text-primary hover:underline">start with a free strategy call</a>.</p>
        
